 "text": "Lough Gur is a small lake in County Limerick, Ireland, settled for nearly six thousand years. Low hills ring it, each tied to a goddess or a god, such as the goddess Aine and the god Donn Firinne. The lake holds the largest stone circle still standing in Ireland, called the Grange. People saw Lough Gur as an entrance to the Otherworld, a gate between this world and the realm of the fairies. Its legends follow a seven-year cycle. Then the water withdraws and a tree rises from the lake bed, its branches under a green cloth, with a woman named Toice Bhrean knitting below. The fairy queen Aine and her enchanted son Geroid Iarla appear at the end of each cycle.",
